        <item>
            <title>0.2</title>
            <pubDate>Tue, 01 Sep 2026 10:47:09 -0700</pubDate>
            <sparkle:version>553</sparkle:version>
            <sparkle:shortVersionString>0.2</sparkle:shortVersionString>
            <sparkle:minimumSystemVersion>26.0</sparkle:minimumSystemVersion>
            <sparkle:hardwareRequirements>arm64</sparkle:hardwareRequirements>
            <description><![CDATA[<style>
:root { color-scheme: light dark; }
body { font-family: -apple-system, 'Helvetica Neue', sans-serif; font-size: 13px; color: #1a1a1a; margin: 12px; }
@media (prefers-color-scheme: dark) { body { color: #e6e6e6; } }
h2 { font-size: 15px; margin: 0 0 8px; } ul { margin: 4px 0 10px; padding-left: 20px; } li { margin-bottom: 3px; }
</style>
<h2>Asobi 0.2 (build 553)</h2>
<ul>
<li><b>Deliver a session to someone from one link.</b> File ▸ <b>Deliver Session Bundle…</b> names the recipient and how long the link should live. Asobi builds the session as light delivery encodes, compresses it, sends it, and puts a link on your clipboard. They paste it into Asobi Review, it downloads, unpacks and opens — and their notes and markup come back to this session, because the link joins them to it. A 24 MB two-clip session travels as about 6 MB.</li>
<li><b>They can ask for a better version of any clip.</b> The delivery travels light on purpose. On the clip they're looking at, <b>Request Higher-Resolution Clip</b> asks your Asobi to re-encode that one clip at full size and send it; it swaps into their session in place, keeping its name, its notes and their marks. You see it re-encoding and uploading; they see a percentage.</li>
<li><b>You choose where bundles live.</b> Settings ▸ <b>Session bundles location</b> — separate from the media cache, because a delivery is a session someone works from rather than something to sweep. It governs both ends: where a delivery you receive unpacks, and where one you send is built and compressed, so a full boot disk never stops a delivery.</li>
<li><b>Revoke a delivery early.</b> The Shared Review panel shows what you sent, to whom and until when, with one button that deletes it from the store. Anyone who already downloaded it keeps their copy.</li>
<li><b>Asobi can read the links it hands out.</b> A short review link pasted into the app now resolves properly — and it carries the recipient's name, so their seat, their watermark and the notes they send back are theirs.</li>
<li><b>Web proxies never upscale, and shapes an edge cap can't see are caught.</b> A 1280×720 source is encoded at 1280×720; a tall side-by-side stack is measured by its pixels, not just its longest edge.</li>
</ul>
]]></description>
